  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nOur approach combines multi-resolution optimization, adaptive gradient-based local search, and population-based methods to escape local optima. The key idea is to adaptively adjust the grid resolution during optimization to better navigate the complex optimization landscape. We begin by fine-grained optimization (n=300) before exploring coarser resolutions. The gradient of the overlap function guides our search, incorporating curvature information and preference for solutions with smooth correlations. This method specifically targets the bottleneck of standard gradient-free optimization by integrating gradient information from all lags and using two-point Lagrangian interpolation to create particle paths. The solution includes both random search initialization and adaptive gradient steps based on evaluation history to avoid getting trapped in suboptimal regions.\n\nMathematical specification:\nVariables: \n- h(x): Discretized step function on [0,2] with n_points samples\n- dx = 2.0 / n_points\n- Overlap metric: C5 = (1/(n_points)) * max_k [||h * (convolution of (1-h) with shift k)||_1 * dx\nConstraints:\n- \u222bh(x)dx = n_points/2\n- 0\u2264h(x)\u22641 for all x\nGradient computation:\n- Active lag identification via top-k selection\n- Second-order gradient estimation using pair-wise difference quotients\n- Dominant lag detection using cumulative sum analysis\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n- Read initial_h_values if provided (n=300)\n- Set global parameters: max_grid=900, min_grid=50\n- Initialize population if parent not available\n\n2. Multi-resolution iteration:\n   for grid_size in [initial_grid, step_sizes]:\n      - Create grid: x_grid = np.linspace(0, 2, grid_size)\n      - Set dx = 2.0/grid_size\n      - Compute initial objective value via correlation\n\n3. Adaptive gradient-based search:\n   Repeat until time exceeded or max_iter reached:\n      - Compute gradients for top 5-20 lags\n      - Apply gradient-adjusted two-point Lagrange interpolation\n      - Maintain gradient memory over last 100 evaluations\n      - Detect stable improvement via Kalman filter\n\n4. Candidate sharing:\n   - Carry best solution upward/downward grids\n   - Use piecewise interpolation for grid transfer\n\n5. Local refinement:\n   Perform non-monotonic line search with damping factors \u03b1=0.1 and \u03b2=0.9\n   Apply feasibility projection after every update\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Base grid: 300 (provided if available)\n- Grid progression: 150, 300, 450, 600, 750, 900\n- Lag buffer: Top 15% lags detected\n- Gradient descent step control:\n   Linear: 0.01 to 0.2\n   0.1 augment damping\n   1.0 momentum coefficient\n- Restart triggers:\n   - Improvement stall (100 iterations)\n   - Gradient norm saturation\n   - Grid monotonicity failure\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Base case n=300: 6 evaluations/second\n- Monitoring: Every 50 iterations\n- Deadline management: Pre-allocate grid progression\n- Fallback: Linear interpolation from base solution\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Verify 0\u2264h\u22641 via clipping (axiom)\n2. Validate \u2211h = grid_size/2 (axiom)\n3. Compute C5 via correlated convolution (exact match)\n4. Return formatted tuple (h, C5, grid_size)",
